from tn import *
def C(ch,*a,**k): TN(9,"science",ch,*a,**k)
C("sound","Asha clapped near the hill behind her school and heard her clap again a moment later. \"That is an echo,\" said her teacher.",
 ["Sound is produced by vibrating bodies and needs a medium to travel, so it cannot pass through a vacuum. It travels as a longitudinal wave, a disturbance with compressions and rarefactions. The speed of sound is different in solids, liquids and gases.",
  "A sound wave has a time period, a frequency (vibrations per second), a wavelength, an amplitude and a speed. Loudness depends on the amplitude, pitch depends on the frequency and timbre is the quality that tells two instruments apart even when loudness and pitch are the same.",
  "Sound with a frequency below 20 Hz is infrasonic and above 20,000 Hz is ultrasonic. Sound reflects from hard surfaces, and an echo is heard when the reflected sound reaches us after at least about 0.1 second. Reverberation is the persistence of sound from repeated reflections.",
  "Ultrasonic sound is used in SONAR to find the depth of the sea and in medical scans, and the electrocardiogram records the electrical activity of the heart."],
 ["longitudinal wave","frequency","amplitude","timbre","echo","ultrasonic sound"],
 ("A wave has frequency 500 Hz and wavelength 0.68 m. Find its speed.",["Speed = frequency x wavelength.","500 x 0.68 = 340.","Speed = 340 m/s."]),
 ("Sound can travel through a vacuum.",["Sound needs a medium of particles.","In a vacuum there is nothing to vibrate."]),
 ["Sound is a longitudinal wave needing a medium.","Loudness depends on amplitude, pitch on frequency.","Echo is reflected sound.","Ultrasound has frequency above 20,000 Hz."],
 ["m|Sound travels as a|longitudinal wave|light ray|stream of electrons|Compressions and rarefactions.","t|Sound cannot travel in a vacuum.|T|It needs a medium.","m|Loudness depends on|amplitude|wavelength|colour|Bigger amplitude, louder sound.","t|Sound with frequency above 20,000 Hz is infrasonic.|F|That is ultrasonic.","m|The number of vibrations per second is|frequency|amplitude|timbre|Unit hertz.","s|What do we call reflected sound heard later?|echo|An echo.","m|SONAR uses|ultrasonic waves|X-rays|radio signals|To find depth under water.","t|Pitch depends on frequency.|T|Higher frequency, higher pitch."])
C("universe","On a clear night Raju saw a milky band across the sky. \"That is our galaxy, the Milky Way,\" said his grandfather.",
 ["The universe is believed to have begun about 13.7 billion years ago with a huge explosion, the Big Bang. The part we can observe is called the observable universe. Stars are grouped in galaxies, and our galaxy is the Milky Way, so named because it appears as a milky band of light. Groups of stars that form patterns are constellations.",
  "The solar system has the Sun at the centre, which is a star, with eight planets going round it along paths called orbits. The time for one revolution is the period of revolution and the time for one rotation is the period of rotation. The four inner planets are rocky or terrestrial, and the four outer planets, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune, are gas giants.",
  "Other bodies of the solar system are moons, asteroids, comets and meteors. Kepler's laws describe how planets move around the Sun, and an object orbiting the Earth needs a certain orbital velocity. The International Space Station is a laboratory in orbit built by many nations working together."],
 ["Big Bang","galaxy","solar system","orbit","Kepler's laws","International Space Station"],
 ("Name the four outer planets.",["Planets beyond the asteroid belt are the outer planets.","They are large and made of gas.","Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune."]),
 ("The Earth is at the centre of the universe.",["Early people thought so before telescopes.","The Earth is one planet going round the Sun, in one galaxy among many."]),
 ["The universe began about 13.7 billion years ago.","The Milky Way is our galaxy.","Inner planets are rocky, outer are gaseous.","The ISS is an international laboratory in space."],
 ["m|Our galaxy is called the|Milky Way|Andromeda|Sombrero|It looks like a milky band.","t|The Sun is a star.|T|Yes.","m|A planet's path round the Sun is its|orbit|axis|crust|It is a curved path.","t|Jupiter is a rocky planet.|F|It is a gas giant.","m|The ISS is|an orbiting laboratory|a comet|a moon|Built by many countries.","s|Name the explosion believed to have begun the universe. Two words.|Big Bang|The Big Bang.","m|Time to complete one revolution is the period of|revolution|rotation|gravity|Around the Sun.","t|Groups of stars forming patterns are constellations.|T|Yes."])
C("matter-around-us","Meena stirred sugar into water and then sand into water. \"One disappears, the other settles,\" she said.",
 ["Matter is classified into pure substances and mixtures. An atom is the smallest unit of an element that takes part in a chemical reaction. A molecule is the smallest unit of a pure substance that can exist independently. When two or more elements combine chemically, the new substance is a compound.",
  "A mixture can be homogeneous, where the parts cannot be seen separately, or heterogeneous, where they can. Mixtures are separated by methods suited to the parts: sublimation, solvent extraction, simple and fractional distillation, centrifugation and chromatography.",
  "A solution is a homogeneous mixture in which the component in the smaller amount is the solute and the one in the larger amount is the solvent. Solutions may be solid, liquid or gas. Suspensions and colloids are heterogeneous mixtures whose particles are bigger."],
 ["atom and molecule","compound","homogeneous mixture","distillation","chromatography","solute and solvent"],
 ("In salt water which is the solute and which the solvent?",["The solute is present in the smaller amount.","Salt is the solute.","Water is the solvent."]),
 ("A compound is just a mixture of elements.",["A compound is formed by a chemical combination of elements.","A mixture keeps the properties of its parts."]),
 ["Matter is pure substance or mixture.","Mixtures can be homogeneous or heterogeneous.","Choose the separation method by the property of the parts.","A solution has a solute and a solvent."],
 ["m|The smallest unit of a pure substance that can exist alone is a|molecule|proton|solute|It keeps the properties of the substance.","t|A solution is a homogeneous mixture.|T|Yes.","m|Separating two liquids with different boiling points uses|fractional distillation|filtering|sublimation|Different boiling points.","t|Sand in water is a homogeneous mixture.|F|The sand can be seen separately.","m|In a solution the component in smaller amount is the|solute|solvent|filtrate|Like sugar in tea.","s|Which method separates dyes in ink?|chromatography|It separates by movement on paper.","m|A substance formed by chemically combining elements is a|compound|mixture|suspension|Fixed ratio.","t|Centrifugation separates fine solids from a liquid.|T|Using a centrifuge."])
C("atomic-structure","Raju saw a model of an atom with a tiny centre and circling dots. \"Is the atom like a small solar system?\" he asked.",
 ["Rutherford fired alpha particles at a thin gold foil and discovered that an atom has a tiny, dense, positively charged nucleus. His model had limits, so Bohr proposed that electrons revolve in fixed paths called orbits or shells, and the shell nearest the nucleus, the K shell, has the least energy.",
  "The number of protons in an atom is its atomic number Z and the sum of protons and neutrons is its mass number A. Electrons are arranged in shells as the electronic configuration, such as 2, 8, 3 for aluminium. The outermost shell is the valence shell, and the combining capacity of an element is its valency.",
  "Atoms of the same element with different mass numbers are isotopes, atoms with the same mass number but different atomic numbers are isobars, and atoms with the same number of neutrons are isotones. The chapter also gives the laws of chemical combination such as the law of multiple proportions."],
 ["nucleus","Bohr's model","atomic number","mass number","valency","isotopes and isobars"],
 ("Write the electronic configuration of an atom with 13 electrons.",["Fill the K shell with 2.","Fill the L shell with 8.","The remaining 3 go to the M shell: 2, 8, 3."]),
 ("The atom is mostly the nucleus.",["Rutherford found the nucleus is tiny.","Most of the atom is empty space where electrons move."]),
 ["Atomic number = protons.","Mass number = protons + neutrons.","Electrons fill shells K, L, M in order.","Isotopes share the atomic number but not the mass number."],
 ["m|Rutherford used a foil of|gold|iron|copper|Gold is very malleable.","t|The nucleus is positively charged.|T|It holds the protons.","m|Atomic number is the number of|protons|neutrons|molecules|Z.","t|The M shell is closest to the nucleus.|F|The K shell is closest.","m|Atoms with same Z but different A are|isotopes|isobars|isotones|Same element.","s|What is the outermost shell called?|valence shell|It holds valence electrons.","m|The electronic configuration of aluminium is|2, 8, 3|2, 8, 8|2, 3, 8|13 electrons in all.","t|Valency is the combining capacity of an element.|T|Yes."])
